Hi [~accountid:712020:a7a7d4bc-10ca-44a0-a22e-fa8752330abd] - can we have a sync on this over the coming days so that I can get up-to-speed on how to handle LERs (as I confess this is my first!).

----

I have reviewed the [Confluence page|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LEG/pages/3883893222/Engagement+with+law+enforcement] and understand that:

* We can share data with law enforcement where failing to do so would impede the requestor's ability to undertake a lawful duty connected with the purposes of the prevention or detection of crime or apprehension or prosecution of offenders (which appears to be the case here);
* The request must be in writing, with an official letterhead and an appropriate signature (again, this seems to be the case here);
* The requestor must provide information on why the information is being requested (i.e., there must be some further context to the crime itself, whereas here the Border Guard has just said they want the data in connection with pre-trial proceedings);
* The request must contain the case number, the investigator’s name, rank, signature and contact information (all of these included except an email address / obvious instructions on how we should respond);
* The request must contain details of the individual (it does);
* The request must refer to applicable legal provisions (it does, although I am no expert on these laws - do we typically review them ourselves?);
* The scope of the data request must be reasonable (I am asking CS to clarify this, as it is something related to a user’s passport); and
* We will only share the minimum information necessary.

----

In light of the above, it looks like the next steps are as follows - {color:#4c9aff}*is this correct*{color}? If so then I would be happy to push this forward:

# Confirm if we have received such requests before, and if so what channel did we use to respond?
# Once confirmed, ask the Border Guard for more details on why they need this information (i.e., anything they can tell us about the allegation / crime / proceedings). Also, if CS are unable to clarify the exact nature of the information being requested, we should clarify this too with the Border Guard.
# Once we have more clarity, provide the minimum info that is necessary to satisfy the request.
